WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.920
I used to complain about work all the time.&nbsp;
The long hours, the disrespect from my boss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.200
getting home exhausted, aching, and sunburned.&nbsp;
I worked in construction a lifetime ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.000
I’d give anything to go back to that now. What’s&nbsp;
that they always say? You don’t know what you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:22.320
got until it’s gone? Maybe I deserve the hand I’ve&nbsp;
been dealt. I haven’t been a good person. I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:27.200
play well with others. There was an argument&nbsp;
with my foreman, one that got a little too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.160
heated, and I just couldn’t reign in my temper.
The hammer I’d been using all day was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.120
still in my hand…but I digress.
I used to build things with my hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:42.880
but I’m in a different line of work now. If you&nbsp;
can call it work. That would imply I get paid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.160
I clock in every morning, and I get to go&nbsp;
home afterward. I don’t. I’m a prisoner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.240
really. A glorified lab rat, and property&nbsp;
of the all-powerful SCP Foundation. I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.840
been here about three weeks, and everyone always&nbsp;
says D-Classes like us last about a month here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.200
So, the clock is ticking down to zero for me.&nbsp;
I haven’t had to do anything too dangerous yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:11.040
but like I said. It’s just a matter of time.
I’m sitting in my cell, the same windowless box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.680
I’ve spent every day for the past three weeks,&nbsp;
when a guard bursts in and grabs me by the arm.&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.440
“What’s going on?” I ask, but he ignores me.
Of course, why make conversation with a lab rat?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.600
Better not to think of us as human at all.
He yanks me down the hall, opens a door,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.400
and shoves me inside. There are several other&nbsp;
D-Class in there already, and they’re staring at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:35.120
something in the corner that I can’t quite&nbsp;
make out. I start to turn around, to take in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.160
the rest of the room, but a voice comes&nbsp;
over the loudspeaker, cold and clinical.&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.400
“Look at the statue in the corner.&nbsp;
Do not take your eyes off it.”&nbsp;

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:49.120
Well, when a disembodied voice at the SCP&nbsp;
Foundation tells you to look at something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.160
you’d better do it. So, I look.
It's a sculpture, made from concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.240
and rebar. It looks pretty harmless, but I’ve&nbsp;
heard enough screams through the walls to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.120
know that they don’t keep too many harmless things&nbsp;
caged up here. Whatever this thing really is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.160
I don’t want to know what it’ll do to me when&nbsp;
my back is turned. I stare at it, unblinking,&nbsp;

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.560
feeling my eyes burn and tear up from the&nbsp;
dry air and concentration. I’m scared to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:16.880
close my eyes for even a second, determined&nbsp;
not to be the first one in here to break.&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:23.440
But something out there has other plans for&nbsp;
me, for us. I hear a warbling sound like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:29.520
motor struggling to start, the sudden crash of&nbsp;
thunder outside, and then…the room goes black.&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.440
For a second I think I’ve gone blind, but then&nbsp;
I hear the other guys screaming, the yelling of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.840
scientists behind the wall. Somehow, the&nbsp;
power’s gone out. I can’t see anything,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.480
but I can hear the sound of stone scraping&nbsp;
against itself, the snap of breaking bones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:47.840
the thud of a limp body slumping to the floor.
The room comes into focus again, as a backup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:53.040
generator kicks in and fills the space with weak&nbsp;
fluorescent light. But something’s different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.240
something’s wrong. The statue in the corner&nbsp;
is gone. The other two men are on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.000
heads twisted around backwards.&nbsp;
And the door is standing wide open.&nbsp;

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:07.440
Is this a trap? A trick of some kind? If I run out&nbsp;
that door, will a guard be waiting there for me?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:11.360
I don’t know, but whatever might happen&nbsp;
to me if I try can’t be any worse than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.880
what will happen if I stay right here.
Through the wall, I hear that sickening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:20.720
snapping sound again, a corpse thudding to the&nbsp;
ground. They didn’t plan this. Something in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.480
their experiment went horribly wrong, for them.&nbsp;
And maybe, just maybe, it went right for me.&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.560
I don’t give it a second thought, I’m out&nbsp;
the door, running through the dimly lit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.800
halls, through the maze of identical&nbsp;
corridors in search of some kind of exit.&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.080
All around me, there’s chaos. Guards firing&nbsp;
their weapons at inhuman shapes that I catch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.720
a brief glimpse of as I run past, scientists&nbsp;
yelling for help, people going as fast as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:49.440
they can toward the danger and away from it.
I’m so caught up in it all, I don’t even notice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.840
when I run right into a woman in a white&nbsp;
lab coat, knocking us both to the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:57.840
I scramble to my feet, and look at her with&nbsp;
wide eyes, waiting for her to turn me in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.560
to call the guards. But she doesn’t.
“I’m so sorry,” I stammer.&nbsp;

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.440
She holds out a hand, gesturing for me to help&nbsp;
her to her feet. So, I do. “Watch your step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.240
there,” she says, with a knowing smile. I&nbsp;
can’t help but notice how beautiful she is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:19.120
twinkling brown eyes and thick black hair. Then, I&nbsp;
spot them, tucked under her hair but unmistakable.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.160
She has a pair of fox ears on top of her&nbsp;
head. It hits me in rapid succession then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:29.360
all the things about this woman that don’t look&nbsp;
quite right. Her unusually long nails, too sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:34.880
and pointed for lab work. The feral glint in&nbsp;
her eyes. The halting cadence of her voice.&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.000
This woman is not an employee&nbsp;
of the SCP Foundation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.320
She’s doing the same thing I am: taking&nbsp;
advantage of the situation to try and escape.&nbsp;

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.040
"I'll be more careful from now on,&nbsp;
ma’am,” I say. She winks and turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.040
down the hall. I can see a tail poking&nbsp;
out beneath the bottom of her lab coat.&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:57.120
“Safe travels!” I call after her. She laughs, a&nbsp;
dangerous sound, like she’s not the one who needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.600
to worry about staying safe, and I wonder what&nbsp;
might have happened if I had caught her in a worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.760
mood. Good thing my mom taught me my manners.
Now, which way should I go from here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.880
Should I follow the woman? I get the&nbsp;
feeling she doesn’t want any company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.120
and I’d prefer to stay on her good side.
As I’m thinking it through, I hear a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.520
popping sound behind me, like a vacuum&nbsp;
in the air being filled all of a sudden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:22.800
I spin around, and there’s a guy standing there,&nbsp;
ordinary as you please. Blonde hair, green eyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:28.240
wearing jeans, and a t-shirt that says “Mothman&nbsp;
Fan Club” in red letters. This guy looks pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.800
out of place, more the type that you’d see&nbsp;
browsing a comic book store, but after my prior&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:39.200
strange encounter I know not to assume anything.
“Hey man, what’s going on?” He asks me. I stare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:44.640
back, not sure what to say. “What did I miss?”&nbsp;
He continues. “I feel like I was gone for ages.”&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.960
“Um,” I gesture to our collective&nbsp;
surroundings, the sound of alarms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:53.200
blaring and voices crying out for backup, the&nbsp;
inhuman shrieks of creatures being forced back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.920
into their cells. “There’s a lot going on.”
“Huh, yeah. Seems like it,” the guy sighs.&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.080
“You wouldn’t happen to know how to get&nbsp;
out of here, would you?” I ask, hopefully.&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.280
He shakes his head.
“Sorry, I don’t really come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.040
and go through the door here. Guess I’ll&nbsp;
just go back to my room until things calm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.560
down a bit. Do you want anything? I’ve&nbsp;
got a mini-fridge in there,” he offers.&nbsp;

00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:17.760
“…No thanks.”
“Suit yourself, man!”&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:22.800
He shoves his hands in his pockets, and walks&nbsp;
away, whistling a tune to himself. If he’s this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.960
unphased by teleporting into the middle&nbsp;
of a complete madhouse, I don’t even want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.960 --> 00:06:31.200
to know what kind of stuff he’s seen. Nice&nbsp;
enough kid, though. Probably better company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:35.360
than anyone, or anything, else in this place.
If I’m not going to take him up on his offer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:39.120
though, I should keep moving before someone&nbsp;
notices I’m not where I’m supposed to be.&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:43.040
I round a corner just in time to see&nbsp;
another body drop. I whirl around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:49.120
looking for that horrible statue, but I don’t&nbsp;
see it. Instead, I see what looks like a man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:54.400
dressed in all black, wearing a mask in the shape&nbsp;
of a bird’s face with a long, pointed beak. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.560
carrying an old-school doctor’s bag, and he’s&nbsp;
bending down to inspect the body of the guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:03.600
that just keeled over. I should keep running,&nbsp;
I should do something, but I can’t look away.&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.560
The man in black pulls a syringe full of thick&nbsp;
green liquid from his bag and injects it into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:13.520
corpse. To my horror, the body begins to move,&nbsp;
thrashing around, hands opening and closing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:19.920
grasping for something. It sits up, reanimated,&nbsp;
but…wrong. Its eyes are bulging and vacant like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:25.840
a goldfish, its mouth hangs slack. As the zombie&nbsp;
climbs to its feet, the figure in all-black turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.120
to look at me, eyes shining from beneath his mask.
“Hello, my good fellow! How are you feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:34.400
today?” He calls to me, his&nbsp;
voice polished and polite.&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.080
“I’m fine!” I shout, my hands&nbsp;
shaking, my chest tight.&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:41.040
The doctor tilts his head&nbsp;
to the side thoughtfully.&nbsp;

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:46.400
“You don’t look well, my friend. Perhaps you could&nbsp;
allow me to examine you?” The zombie shuffles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:51.440
toward me, and the doctor opens his bag. “There’s&nbsp;
a pestilence raging through this land, you see.”&nbsp;

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.240
I’m not going to stick around to find&nbsp;
out what that exam might look like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:58.960
or what he might do in the service of curing me.
“No thanks, I don’t have health insurance!”&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.840
That is all I can think to say as I turn and run&nbsp;
back the way I came. I still have no idea where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.800
an exit might be, how I might get out of here. It&nbsp;
all looks the same, one long stretch of tile and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:12.720
white. I guess a place like the Foundation&nbsp;
isn’t going to have red neon signs telling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.080
you how to escape. But this place can’t go on&nbsp;
forever…can it? I’m starting to have my doubts.&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.880
Before I can get too lost in my own hopelessness,&nbsp;
the biggest man I’ve ever seen comes lumbering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.400
down the hall toward me. I worked with&nbsp;
some pretty big guys in construction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.840
massive behemoths who would carry steel beams like&nbsp;
they were pool noodles, but this man is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:37.120
else entirely. He’s gotta be…eight feet tall?&nbsp;
With fists that look like they could crush my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:40.800
skull in one hit. I’d better get out of this&nbsp;
monster’s way if I know what’s good for me.&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:47.280
But as he passes me, he waves and gives me a&nbsp;
great big friendly - if unnaturally wide - smile.&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:54.720
“Beautiful day, isn’t it?” he bellows in&nbsp;
a booming, French-accented voice. With his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.840
other hand, he picks at his teeth with something&nbsp;
white. A closer look reveals it to be a shard of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.080
bone. I have to imagine it’s human.&nbsp;
His lips are stained dark with blood.&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:11.200
“You look lost, can I help you with something?”
A helpful people-eater. What a guy. I keep my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.560
distance but decide to try my luck.
“You wouldn’t happen to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.560 --> 00:09:18.480
where the exit is?” I say.
He beams at me, thrilled by my question.&nbsp;

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:23.520
“I’m so glad you asked! It’s that way!” He&nbsp;
points down a hall to the left. “Just follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.240
that hall all the way down, and take the only&nbsp;
right! That should get you where you’re going!”&nbsp;

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:31.200
“Thanks!”
I give him a wave back as I head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:36.640
that way. As I go, I can hear the giant singing&nbsp;
opera to himself, something Italian. It reminds me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:42.400
of a record my grandma used to play, a long time&nbsp;
ago. Maybe this will get me free, maybe it won’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.240
but it’s more information than I had a&nbsp;
second ago. Might as well give it a shot.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:50.000
If I make it through this, I’m going to be in&nbsp;
the best shape of my life from all the running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.560
I’ve done today. Maybe I’ll try to do a marathon.&nbsp;
Or maybe I shouldn’t get ahead of myself.&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:58.400
There’s no one else down this particular&nbsp;
hall, at least no one that I can see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:04.320
I slow my pace to a steady walk, keeping my eyes&nbsp;
and ears alert for any potential threats. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:09.920
so good. I reach the other side of the hall, and&nbsp;
take a right turn…and nothing. Just a wall. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:14.640
giant son of a gun lied to me! I guess that’s&nbsp;
what I get for asking a cannibal for directions.&nbsp;

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:19.040
I guess I’m lucky he just misled me instead of&nbsp;
having me for dessert. I’m about to plan my next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:24.480
move, when I feel something nudge against the&nbsp;
back of my leg. I turn, and there’s what I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:30.400
only describe as a blob. Just a large mass of&nbsp;
orange slime, wrapping around my legs, making a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:35.120
friendly high-pitched gurgle. I should be&nbsp;
suspicious of it, after everything I’ve seen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:39.920
but I’m not. I just get the feeling this&nbsp;
thing has my best interests at heart.&nbsp;

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:44.560
“Hey, buddy…” I pat its slimy&nbsp;
surface, and it ripples delightedly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.360
making another gurgling sound. It’s kind of&nbsp;
cute, honestly. I smell the scent of freshly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:55.280
brewed coffee, one of my favorite smells in&nbsp;
the world. I haven’t had a decent cup in years.&nbsp;

00:10:55.280 --> 00:11:01.360
All of a sudden, I feel this overwhelming sense&nbsp;
of happiness, of hope. Even if it’s foolish to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:05.520
think so, I honestly believe that everything&nbsp;
is going to be alright. This strange little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:10.800
friendly slime has given me the strength to carry&nbsp;
on. I wish I had a plushie version of this thing.&nbsp;

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:15.360
There’s a sudden tickling sensation along my&nbsp;
legs, and I laugh, pushing the slime away gently.&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:19.360
“Hey, stop that!”
It coos apologetically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.680
bumps itself against me again like a stray dog&nbsp;
asking for scratches behind the ears. I wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:28.240
for a second if I could take this thing with me,&nbsp;
but what would I even feed it? What kind of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.400
could I give a domesticated slime creature?&nbsp;
Better to leave it with people who know how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:37.440
to care for it, even if they’re mad scientists.
I give it another pat, then head on my way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:42.560
The slime oozes off in the opposite direction,&nbsp;
off to find more new friends, I imagine. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.800
no idea there was anything nice here, with&nbsp;
everything horrible that I’ve seen today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.960
Warms my cold heart, just a little bit.
It's strange, I’ve been walking for a while,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.960 --> 00:11:56.480
and I haven’t seen anyone else. It’s eerily&nbsp;
quiet over here, I can’t hear a thing except&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.480 --> 00:12:00.800
for that alarm in the distance. Not sure when all&nbsp;
the screaming went quiet, or where all the guards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:06.160
went. It’s peaceful, but I have to admit it’s&nbsp;
eerie. There’s no way it’s this easy. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:10.720
no way I’ll be able to just walk out of here.&nbsp;
Life doesn’t work like that, especially not here.&nbsp;

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:15.280
My eyes dart around, watching for any potential&nbsp;
danger as I go. Maybe all the action is on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.280 --> 00:12:19.600
other side of the facility, maybe something broke&nbsp;
out of containment that’s so big, so dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.600 --> 00:12:23.840
that all hands are on deck to contain it.&nbsp;
Or maybe they’re all dead, and I’m next.&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:29.840
I shouldn’t think like that. I feel the hairs&nbsp;
on the back of my neck prickle, and I have that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.840 --> 00:12:36.240
paranoid feeling of being watched. Like eyes,&nbsp;
burning into me. I glance behind me, but there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:41.840
no one there. I stop walking, but I don’t hear&nbsp;
any other footsteps. Calm down, there’s no one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:46.320
here with you, I tell myself. Just keep moving.
I turn my eyes back to the path ahead of me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.320 --> 00:12:52.080
and I freeze. What was that? I saw something,&nbsp;
just for a second. Something long, thin, spindly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:58.960
like a stick bug but much, much bigger. When I&nbsp;
look for it, it’s gone. But I know that I saw it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:03.200
Or this place has now driven me insane. I glance&nbsp;
to the side, and there it is again! For a split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:07.440
second, so fast I could almost convince myself&nbsp;
I imagined it. If I were at home, or walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:13.120
down an ordinary street, I might think I had.&nbsp;
But I’m not anywhere ordinary. And a slender&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:17.040
monster that can hide in my peripheral vision,&nbsp;
only be spotted for a millisecond at a time?&nbsp;

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.520
That seems par for the&nbsp;
course in a place like this.&nbsp;

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:25.040
There! I just saw it again! It’s like it wants&nbsp;
me to know it’s here, but that I’ll never be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:30.080
able to pin it down. I’m not playing mind games&nbsp;
with a monster, forget it. I’ll keep moving.&nbsp;

00:13:30.080 --> 00:13:34.960
Another flash of the creature, closer than it&nbsp;
was before. “Whatever, you don’t scare me.” I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:39.520
say out loud. I’m lying, but I hope it can’t&nbsp;
tell. If I just play it cool, maybe this thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.520 --> 00:13:43.760
will get bored and find someone else to menace.&nbsp;
I haven’t been running for a long time now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:48.000
but my heart is still pounding. I can&nbsp;
taste metal in the back of my throat.&nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:52.080
Suddenly, all the sounds from before are&nbsp;
back. The gunfire, the screaming, the shriek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:58.240
of alarms. I’ve made my way back to the action.&nbsp;
One sound drowns out all the rest: a deafening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:04.480
roar, animalistic and powerful and…hateful.
It reminds me of a dinosaur in a movie, but with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:09.360
a power that makes my bones rattle and my teeth&nbsp;
chatter. The roar is followed by more screaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:16.320
and a massive crash, the crunch of wood and stone.&nbsp;
There’s another roar, more distant than before.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:20.720
Wait…whatever made that sound, it just broke out.&nbsp;
And I bet it left a pretty big hole in its wake.&nbsp;

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:27.440
I follow the sound and find exactly what I hoped&nbsp;
I would: A massive opening in the wall, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.760
sweet feeling of a breeze coming through.&nbsp;
I couldn’t find an exit, but this thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:36.960
just made one for me. I don’t waste any more time,&nbsp;
I make a break for it, dancing around rubble and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:41.440
pools of corrosive acid, and sprinting into&nbsp;
the nearby forest. I’m ducking under branches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:46.320
darting around trees, and I run until I’m&nbsp;
seeing spots and my lungs are gasping for air.&nbsp;

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:51.040
I don’t know how far I’ll make it, if&nbsp;
there’s a town nearby or any kind of shelter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.040 --> 00:14:56.400
But I promise one thing, if I survive, I’m going&nbsp;
to be a better person. I’ll get it right this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:01.040
time. I’ll earn my second chance at life.
Now go check out “How to Work at the SCP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.040 --> 00:15:07.200
Foundation Explained” and “Revenge of D&nbsp;
Class Ghosts SCP-4973 - Dead Men Walking”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:11.920
for more looks at the lives (and&nbsp;
deaths) of SCP Foundation D-Class!

